{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://aureline.dev/schemas/security/m5-notification-activity-handoff.schema.json",
  "title": "Aureline M5 notification / activity-center handoff routing primitive: durable, reopenable advisory and revocation routing that never collapses to badge-only, toast-only, or website-only across activity-center, native-notification, Help/About, and support-bundle channels",
  "description": "Export-safe packet implementing Aureline's one reusable notification / activity-center handoff routing primitive so a security-affecting advisory or revocation event stays visible through every claimed M5 route — a durable activity-center row, a privacy-safe native OS notification where policy allows, a Help/About summary, and a support-bundle export field — instead of collapsing to a bare badge, a transient toast that disappears, or a link to an external page. Each notification-delivery row binds the shared handoff anatomy (event identity, severity, affected scope, current status, delivery state, reopen target, and primary action — all carried by every route without a detail drawer), the severity classes, action states, required actions, continuity claims, delivery profiles, mirror-freshness states, native-notification behaviors, export fields, and accessibility routes reused verbatim from the frozen M5 advisory-component matrix, the four channels (activity center, native notification, Help/About, support bundle) that render the handoff in parity, the event kinds it can route, and the focus behaviors that keep the delivery state, the affected scope, and the reopen action keyboard-reachable. Each lane carries worked resolution cases: a resolver input (one advisory / revocation event on one notification-delivery lane with its copy-safe advisory id, severity, event kind, affected scope, current status, authoritative reopen surface, reopen target, signer / source state, delivery profile, mirror freshness, action state, primary action, and continuity claim) and the resolved handoff — the derived delivery posture (a suppressed OS notification still lands durably in the activity center rather than collapsing to a badge, and an emergency-grade severity bypasses quiet hours / do-not-disturb), the reopen surface (every notification and activity row lands on the authoritative affected-install or disclosure surface, never a dead-end), the privacy-safe native-notification behaviors, the always-durable and always-visible posture, the per-channel projections that share one advisory vocabulary, and the copy-safe export summary — where the stored resolution must equal a fresh resolve of its input. Hard invariants forbid collapsing an event to a badge-only, toast-only, or website-only state, hiding handoff truth behind a detail drawer, dropping an event out of the durable activity history, splitting the native-notification and activity-row vocabulary, and dropping the copy-safe id or export summary. The derived delivery posture and reopen surface are resolver-side vocabularies, kept out of the frozen set. The shell zone, responsive class, window class, and consumer surface vocabularies are reused verbatim from the frozen M5 shell-zone matrix; the severity class, action state, required action, continuity claim, delivery profile, mirror-freshness state, native-notification behavior, export field, accessibility route, qualification class, and downgrade trigger vocabularies are reused verbatim from the frozen M5 advisory-component matrix; the copy-safe advisory id aligns to the frozen advisory-identity id family. Raw hostnames, raw absolute paths, raw exploit payloads, raw signatures, private registry URLs, credentials, and raw notification bodies never cross this boundary; only opaque export-safe reprs and a copy-safe advisory id are carried. The Rust validator and resolver in crates/aureline-shell are the authoritative gate; this schema documents the shape.",
